<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Erbach</h1><p class="st-meta">1532 CE – 1806 CE · capital Erbach</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Odenwald county of the Schenks of Erbach, whose castle holds a famous collection of antiquities and whose town became known for ivory carving.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The lords of Erbach served as hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cupbearer">cupbearers</a> (Schenken) of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate</a> and built up lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odenwald">Odenwald</a> from the 12th century. In 1532 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Charles V</a> raised them to imperial counts, and their territory became the County of Erbach.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> in the 1540s and ruled a wooded upland territory of small towns and villages, often divided among branches such as Erbach-Fürstenau, Erbach-Erbach and Erbach-Schönberg. At the end of the 18th century Count Franz I gathered antiquities, armour and antlers at Erbach castle and in 1783 founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>-carving craft that became the town's speciality.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1806 the county was mediatised and placed under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse</a>. The counts kept their castles, and Erbach is still known for its carving museum and craft.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 1532 CE – 1806 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:90.19%;width:5.45%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1532 CE – 1806 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Erbach</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Franz I of Erbach-Erbach</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Erbach</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1669&amp;focus=county-of-erbach" aria-label="County of Erbach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4910 488 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Erbach lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5230 744L5229 694M5230 744L5198 698"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5198 698h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5230 744h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5230 744h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Erbach. <a href="/atlas?year=1669&amp;focus=county-of-erbach">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
